cinema history is full of dazzling  technical achievements and masterful  editing but some of the most impressive  sequences of all time are shot in one  long fluid take actors and crew members  practice the elaborate sequence until  it's second nature rehearsing every line  in every camera movement until they're  able to execute a gorgeous sequence in a  single unbroken shot here are a few of  the best examples of the form Goodfellas  the films of Martin Scorsese are  absolutely loaded with iconic shots but  few are as well remembered as this  gorgeous long take from Goodfellas known  as the Copa shot it's a nearly  three-minute Steadicam take that follows  Henry and Karen as they enter the  Copacabana nightclub through side  entrance so Henry can flaunt his many  mob base connections  according to camera operator Larry  McConkey the shot was originally just a  simple case of the camera following the  actors but he and cinematographer  Michael Ball Haas decided to spice up  the blocking they did this in part  because McConkey needed pauses in the  walk so he could transition between wide  shots and close-ups without cutting away  that's why they added touches like a  walk through the club's kitchen in the  moment when Henry stops to playfully  chastise a flirting couple Creed nearly  40 years after the first rocky film the  franchise revitalizer Creed delivered a  dazzling uninterrupted fight sequence  for the fight between Adonis and Leo the  Lion spirito director Ryan Coogler  wanted to achieve the visual metaphor of  the two boxers being suddenly all on  their own after months of working with  their trainers so the fight was filmed  as one continuous Steadicam take with  the camera dancing around the actors  Kubler shot 13 takes of the fight with  no additional cameras on set to provide  extra coverage should he later decide to  break it up in the editing room  the result is an intense intimate  sequence that ranks among the best  moments in boxing cinema since  Scorsese's Raging Bull Children of Men  the acclaimed four-minute car attack  scene in 2006 as children of men was  shot with a special rig that not only  allowed the camera to move around within  the cramped car but move out of it later  there are a lot of moving parts in this  scene to make things even more stressful  director Alfonso Cuaron revealed during  a 2013 San Diego comic-con panel that he  only had approximately four takes  available to get the shot right it ended  up coming down  the very last take before the production  was going to lose access to the location  and korone almost ruined that one  himself we were shooting the last take  everything goes great but then by  accident the blood spills onto the lens  I yelled cut but there was an explosion  and nobody heard me so they kept  shooting then later I realized that the  blood splash was the miracle the final  take the one for own almost a banded mid  shot is the one in the film the player  Robert Altman was one of the most  famously playful directors of the 20th  century the player a dark satire about a  ruthless Hollywood executive is  essentially a movie about movies so  Altman took every chance possible to  cram in references to classic films he  also took the opportunity to give us the  entire look and feel of the film in an  unbroken eight-minute opening shot  beginning with the tongue-in-cheek  clicking of a film slate the tape pulls  back and rises up into a wide crane shot  in an homage to Orson Welles as Touch of  Evil  then the camera dances around the lot  following various characters and nodding  to several cinema classics even if  you're a true movie buff it'll take you  a few tries to catch every reference and  inside joke in the uncut sequence must  be crazy except the coke bottles now  television actress exactly right it's  out of Africa meets pretty woman the  protector though the final product often  looks amazing shooting heavily edited  fight scenes like this taken three  sequence can be tedious work with tons  of close-ups of hands feet and faces or  you can do with creators of 2005's tie  action classic the protector did shoot a  badass 4-minute fight scene featuring  one hero one camera dozens of stuntmen  and lots of stairs in a single amazing  take in this sequence the hero has made  his way to a restaurant specializing in  exotic meats where he believes a  gangster is holding his beloved  elephants proving just how far one man  will go for his animal friends Tom  storms the restaurants VIP area where  he's confronted by an enormous spiral  staircase and a load of henchmen ready  to take him out the result is one of the  best fight sequences of the past two  decades plus it has arguably the best  action hero closing line in the history  of cinema  thanks for watching click the looper  icon to subscribe to our YouTube channel  plus check out all this cool stuff we  know you'll love to  [Music]
 

